 Axios Future of Defense 
 
 
 
 
 By Colin Demarest &middot; Sep 02, 2026 
 
 
 Welcome to September, the official start of Defense Conference Season™️.

 What's your favorite event? Least favorite? Where should I head next? 🎁 Situational awareness: American Rheinmetall yesterday said it delivered to the U.S. Army its first Lynx XM30 prototype. More of the combat vehicles will be handed over this year.

 Up for grabs: Dan Driscoll's exit, the multibillion-dollar Achilles Shield and BWXT's plans for Fort Campbell.

 Today's newsletter is 1,837 words, a 7-minute read. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Flummoxed by F/A-XX 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 August came and went, and still no contract for the U.S. Navy's futuristic fighter, F/A-XX , was announced.

 Why it matters: The date keeps slipping — inspiring memes, leaving defense industry giants in public limbo, pressuring the service's existing fleet and reminding military watchers of the Trump administration's warplane priority.

 Driving the news: Chief of Naval Operations Adm. Daryl Caudle in April told reporters the F/A-XX builder would be selected in August . It's now Sept. 2.

 A CNO spokesperson told me yesterday there was no "additional information to provide on the F/A-XX program or its timing at this point." Aviation Week on Aug. 22 reported that the Navy was "still hopeful an announcement will be made," but noted that it was "out of the service's hands." State of play: Boeing and Northrop Grumman, the sixth- and fourth-largest defense contractors in the world, have been quietly competing for the contract. (Lockheed Martin, the world's largest, exited a while ago .)

 F/A-XX is meant to succeed the Super Hornet, long relied upon and recently employed across the Middle East. Boeing and Northrop have expressed to me confidence in their designs and setups. Both companies referred my inquiries this week to the Navy. Between the lines: Boeing previously won the Air Force's Next Generation Air Dominance fighter competition and is building the sixth-generation F-47.

 Trump 2.0 has elevated that aircraft — named in part for the 47th president — and has maintained that industry can't handle both endeavors at the same time. Yes, but: Steve Parker, the Boeing Defense, Space and Security chief executive, this summer pushed back on that narrative ahead of the Farnborough International Airshow.

 "When we bid what is now the F-47 program and the F/A-XX program, we bid them knowing we had to be able to execute them both," he told reporters in London. "We're excited for the decision; we'll see which way it goes," he added. "Not sure the time frame, but we'll be ready." Follow the money: The White House's $1.5 trillion budget blueprint for fiscal 2027 included $140 million for F/A-XX.

