 3. Exclusive: Aitan emerges 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Aitan today emerged from stealth, backed by tens of millions of dollars and, by its own tally, hundreds of thousands of hours operating alongside the Israel Defense Forces.

 Why it matters: The startup is offering "robotic sovereignty as a service" and "edge AI weapon systems" — giving governments and militaries a backbone to build out their unmanned and autonomous forces quickly and with the lessons of the Middle East and Eastern Europe already digested.

 "Warfare has totally transformed over the past few years," co-founder Daniel Almog told me. "In robotics, the adversary is constantly adapting. The radio-frequency environment is adapting, countermeasures changing. Even the batteries are decaying." Driving the news: Aitan's reveal coincides with a $41 million funding round led by Deep33 and Dell Technologies Capital. The startup, based in New Jersey, has raised $54 million in equity to date.

 "We believe physical AI will be one of the most important technology shifts of the coming decade. But first, we need to solve the challenge of coordinating highly complex autonomous systems in challenging real-world environments," Yair Snir, managing director at Dell Technologies Capital , said in a statement to Axios. "They recognized early on that the future of defense belongs to intelligent networks of disparate systems operating together." Follow the money: Aitan has secured contracts abroad for command-and-control networks and robotic weapon designs.

 "A lot of countries look for battle-tested systems," co-founder Udi Oster told me. There is, he added, general "skepticism of systems that haven't actually been used in battle." Context: Both Almog and Oster served in the IDF's Unit 8200, known for intelligence and cyberspace operations.

 The startup is named in honor of Oster's younger brother, Eitan, who was killed in combat while serving with the Egoz commando unit in Lebanon . Almog and Oster told me his death made their mission — building robotic systems that keep troops out of harm's way — deeply personal. What's next: Aitan is expanding its team in the U.S. and eyeing European and Asian markets.

 Go deeper: Israel wants to deploy its Iron Beam within a year 

 
 
