 4. Shields up 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Hellenic Air Force F-16s fly over the Parthenon and Acropolis in March 2025. Photo: Theophile Bloudanis/AFP via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Israel will build Greece a missile-and-drone defense network similar to its own .

 Why it matters: The arrangement, dubbed Achilles Shield, is valued at around €3 billion, or almost $3.5 billion. It's billed as the largest defense export in the history of Israel-Greece relations.

 Driving the news: The deal was signed Monday in Tel Aviv. It includes:

 David's Sling, Spyder and Barak MX air-defense systems from Rafael and Israel Aerospace Industries; Multi-mission radars from Elta Systems, a subsidiary of IAI ; And a new command-and-control system, to be developed by Rafael. What they're saying: The two countries "share common strategic interests and face shared regional challenges" and plan to "deepen defense cooperation," Israel's defense minister, Israel Katz, said in a statement. 

 He visited Greece a few months ago. Zoom out: Greece is overhauling its military and stimulating its domestic defense industry in an effort known as Agenda 2030.

 More than a dozen Greek firms are participating in Achilles Shield, according to the government. Go deeper: Defense industry must collaborate more in Middle East, says Dunford 

 
 
